C = [
("h1", "9. The Mountain and the Question: Matthew 17 and Mark 9"),
("p", "Claim: on the way down from the mountain the disciples ask about Elias, and the Lord answers with two sentences that look in opposite directions. “Elias truly shall first come, and restore all things” is in the future. “Elias is come already, and they knew him not” is in the past. Mark gives the same pair in the present and the past. The chapter reads the whole scene in order, the mountain, the question and the answer, and records what each sentence says before the next chapter asks what the pair means together."),
("sub", "9.1 Elias on the mountain"),
("p", "Six days after the confession at Caesarea Philippi, “Jesus taketh Peter, James, and John his brother, and bringeth them up into an high mountain apart, and was transfigured before them: and his face did shine as the sun, and his raiment was white as the light” (Matthew 17:1, 2). Then: “And, behold, there appeared unto them Moses and Elias talking with him” (Matthew 17:3). Mark gives the order the other way: “And there appeared unto them Elias with Moses: and they were talking with Jesus” (Mark 9:4). Moses and Elias are named, they are seen and they talk with the Lord. The text does not say how the disciples knew them, and it does not say what was said."),
("p", "Peter answers, “Lord, it is good for us to be here: if thou wilt, let us make here three tabernacles; one for thee, and one for Moses, and one for Elias” (Matthew 17:4). Mark adds, “For he wist not what to say; for they were sore afraid” (Mark 9:6). A voice out of the cloud says, “This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased; hear ye him” (Matthew 17:5), and when the disciples lift their eyes, “they saw no man, save Jesus only” (Matthew 17:8). Moses stands for the law and Elias for the prophets in the common reading, and the text does not say so. The volume notes what is said: two named men, one of whom is Elias, appeared with the Lord on the mountain, and the voice named the Lord alone as the one to hear."),
("sub", "9.2 The charge and the question"),
("p", "“And as they came down from the mountain, Jesus charged them, saying, Tell the vision to no man, until the Son of man be risen again from the dead” (Matthew 17:9). Mark adds that “they kept that saying with themselves, questioning one with another what the rising from the dead should mean” (Mark 9:10). The charge is silence until the resurrection, and the disciples puzzle over the words. Then the question comes: “Why then say the scribes that Elias must first come?” (Matthew 17:10). Mark’s form is “Why say the scribes that Elias must first come?” (Mark 9:11)."),
("p", "The question has a premise and a puzzle. The premise is that the scribes teach that Elias must first come, and the Lord does not dispute the teaching. The puzzle is the word “then.” The disciples have just seen Elias with the Lord, and they have heard the Lord speak of rising from the dead. Their question asks how the scribes’ teaching, that Elias must first come, fits what they have seen. The text does not tell us what they had in mind, and the volume does not guess."),
("sub", "9.3 The two answers"),
("p", "“And Jesus answered and said unto them, Elias truly shall first come, and restore all things” (Matthew 17:11). The verb is future, “shall … come,” and the sentence carries the word “truly” and a second work, “restore all things.” Mark’s form is present: “Elias verily cometh first, and restoreth all things” (Mark 9:12). In both, the Lord affirms the scribes’ teaching. He does not say they are mistaken. He says that Elias comes first and restores all things."),
("p", "Then: “But I say unto you, That Elias is come already, and they knew him not, but have done unto him whatsoever they listed. Likewise shall also the Son of man suffer of them” (Matthew 17:12). Mark’s form: “But I say unto you, That Elias is indeed come, and they have done unto him whatsoever they listed, as it is written of him” (Mark 9:13). The sentence changes the tense and adds an adversative, “But I say unto you.” Elias “is come already.” The verb is past, the people “knew him not,” and what they did to him is what the Son of man will suffer. Then Matthew adds: “Then the disciples understood that he spake unto them of John the Baptist” (Matthew 17:13)."),
("sub", "9.4 What the sentences say"),
("p", "Five features of the text are plain. First, the Lord affirms the scribes’ teaching: Elias “truly shall first come.” Second, he adds a work that is not in Malachi’s wording of 4:5, 6, “restore all things.” Third, he says that Elias “is come already,” and the people “knew him not.” Fourth, what was done to Elias is what the Son of man will suffer. Fifth, the disciples understood the second sentence to be about John the Baptist, and Matthew places that understanding after verse 12, not after verse 11."),
("p", "The two sentences are joined by “But,” and the volume notes what Matthew 17:13 attaches to John. It says that the disciples “understood that he spake unto them of John the Baptist.” The “he spake” has the whole of what the Lord said as its object, and the verse can be read as attaching John to both sentences or to the second. The volume does not decide that on grammar. The difference between the two readings is the subject of the next chapter. Mark’s “as it is written of him” (Mark 9:13) points to Scripture that records what was done to Elias. The nearest written account of suffering for Elijah is in Kings, where Jezebel seeks his life and he says, “I, even I only, am left; and they seek my life, to take it away” (1 Kings 19:10, 14). Mark does not name the passage, and the volume reports the nearest and builds no more."),
("sub", "9.5 Restitution in Acts"),
("p", "The Lord’s phrase “restore all things” has a counterpart in Peter’s sermon in the temple. “Repent ye therefore, and be converted, that your sins may be blotted out, when the times of refreshing shall come from the presence of the Lord; and he shall send Jesus Christ, which before was preached unto you: whom the heaven must receive until the times of restitution of all things, which God hath spoken by the mouth of all his holy prophets since the world began” (Acts 3:19–21). The word “restitution” and the word “restore” are related in the Greek, and the volume reports that fact and does not rest an argument on it. The first volume of the Acts Commentary Series reads the sermon, and this volume cites it. What the passage says is that “the times of restitution of all things” lie ahead of Peter’s hearers, that heaven must receive Jesus until then, and that the prophets spoke of it."),
("ctbl", "The Lord’s two sentences on Elias.", [
    ("", "Matthew 17:11", "Matthew 17:12"),
    ("Tense", "Future: “shall first come”", "Past: “is come already”"),
    ("Work", "“Restore all things”", "“They knew him not … whatsoever they listed”"),
    ("Mark’s form", "Present: “cometh first, and restoreth all things” (9:12)", "“Is indeed come … as it is written of him” (9:13)"),
    ("Link", "“But I say unto you” joins them", "Likewise the Son of man will suffer of them"),
    ("What the disciples understood", "Matthew 17:13 attaches John to the pair", "Said of John the Baptist"),
  ], [0.22, 0.39, 0.39]),
("sub", "9.6 What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "Elias appeared with Moses and the Lord on the mountain, and the voice named the Lord alone as the one to hear.",
    "Asked why the scribes say Elias must first come, the Lord affirms it: “Elias truly shall first come, and restore all things.” Mark has the present, “cometh first, and restoreth all things.”",
    "He then says, “Elias is come already, and they knew him not,” and ties what was done to Elias to what the Son of man will suffer.",
    "Matthew 17:13 says the disciples understood that he spake of John the Baptist, and the volume does not settle on grammar whether that attaches to both sentences.",
    "“Restore all things” has no counterpart in Malachi’s wording, and the nearest text is Acts 3:21, read in the Acts volume and cited here.",
  ]),
]
